success: true
exit_code: 0
----- stdout -----
Soft-reboot into the hidden session (whole-root overlay on the hidden volume)

Usage: nails activate [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -c, --config <PATH>
          Path to the configuration file (overrides binary-relative discovery)

      --no-preflight
          Skip pre-flight checks (DANGEROUS - expert use only)

  -v, --verbose...
          More diagnostics on stderr: -v for debug, -vv for trace

  -y, --yes
          Proceed without the confirmation prompt

          Without this flag activation prompts on a terminal and refuses to run without one, because it logs you out.

      --nixos-flake <FLAKE_REF>
          NixOS: the hidden configuration as a flake reference <path>#<host>

          The hidden system is built from it into the hidden root's store before the soft-reboot. Supports attribute selection after the `#`.

  -q, --quiet
          Print only errors and the final result

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a stream only when it is a terminal, TERM is not `dumb` and NO_COLOR is unset or empty
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour

          [default: auto]

      --dry-run
          Preview activation steps without making changes

      --ascii
          Use ASCII markers instead of Unicode symbols and box drawing

      --overlay-only
          NixOS: enter with the decoy's own system, without building the hidden configuration (the hidden /etc and home still apply)

      --accept-persistent-decoy
          Accept a persistent decoy posture instead of refusing activation

          By default activation refuses a host that keeps forensic traces on the decoy disk: a persistent systemd-journald journal, or an active rsyslog/syslog-ng/auditd. What the decoy logs while it shuts down for the soft-reboot, and while it starts again afterwards, would survive on the decoy disk. This flag accepts that risk (and overrides `accept_persistent_decoy` in `nails.toml`).

      --json
          Print machine-readable JSON on stdout

      --allow-unmounted
          Use a hidden root that is not a volume of its own

          By default activation refuses a root that is a folder rather than a mount point, or that this computer's own storage holds (a subvolume or a bind mount of one of its filesystems, a volume inside its storage, an image file on it): everything the session writes there lands on this computer's disk. This flag turns those refusals into a warning, adds a line to the logout confirmation and records the choice for `status`. A root with a second filesystem mounted over it, storage that cannot be read, and a decoy entry that opens the volume are still refused.

      --no-log-file
          Do not write the log file on the hidden volume (diagnostics still go to stderr)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

WHAT HAPPENS:
  Activation builds one overlay over the whole decoy root at /run/nextroot,
  with every change stored on the hidden volume, and soft-reboots into it
  (systemd 254 or newer). Every program is closed and you are logged out; the
  hidden session starts at its own login screen. Nothing of the hidden session
  can reach the decoy disk: the ESP is read-only, other decoy filesystems are
  overlaid, and decoy swap is never used.

EXAMPLES:
  nails activate                      Enter the hidden session
  nails activate --overlay-only       Enter with the decoy's own system (NixOS)
  nails activate --dry-run            Preview without changing anything

----- stderr -----
